## Method and reasoning
Compute the prefix function in linear time. Its last value is the longest proper border. Any shorter border is also a border of that longest one, so repeatedly follow pi[length−1] until zero. The chain is decreasing, so reverse it for output.

## Walk through the sample
For ababa, the longest proper border is aba, of length 3. Its longest proper border is a, of length 1. Reversing the discovered chain prints lengths 1, 3; the entire string is excluded.

## Complexity
Time and space O(n).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    string s;
    cin >> s;
    int n = s.size();
    vector<int> pi(n), answers;
    for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i) {
        int j = pi[i - 1];
        while (j && s[i] != s[j])
            j = pi[j - 1];
        pi[i] = j + (s[i] == s[j]);
    }
    for (int k = pi.back(); k; k = pi[k - 1])
        answers.push_back(k);
    cout << answers.size() << '\n';
    reverse(answers.begin(), answers.end());
    for (size_t i = 0; i < answers.size(); ++i)
        cout << answers[i] << (i + 1 == answers.size() ? '\n' : ' ');
}
